# The Hebrew (Masoretic) numbering → the Clementine Vulgate's (content/bible/
# vulgate), read by versification.py over its drafts (SIL's org → vul.vrs, and
# TVTMS where the two disagree, settled by the texts). Only where the drafts are
# wrong or silent. `slug c:v[-v] = c:v[-v]` maps a range verse for verse (or
# onto one verse); `= -` marks a Hebrew verse with no counterpart.
#
# How each block was checked: versification.py scores every mapped verse by the
# content words its TAHOT English glosses share with the Douay at the address,
# lists the weak ones, and aligns the chapters with several weak verses on the
# texts. Each block below was then settled by reading the Hebrew glosses beside
# the Douay (and the Latin where the Douay prints its own numbers: Psalms 15,
# 115, 147, Isaias 45–46), verse by verse.

# genesis 5: the Vulgate's 5:31 holds both Lamech's death and Noe's sons
genesis 5:32 = 5:31

# exodus 39: the Vulgate tells the priestly vestments more briefly and runs two
# verses behind from the breastpiece's chains to the inventory (aligned on the
# texts, then read: 39:21 the violet cord = 39:19, 39:27 the tunics = 39:25,
# 39:33 the tabernacle brought = 39:32, 39:39 the bronze altar = 39:39 again)
exodus 39:17 = 39:16
exodus 39:18-19 = 39:17
exodus 39:20-28 = 39:18-26
exodus 39:29-38 = 39:28-37

# numbers 15: the Vulgate makes one verse of "one law and one judgment"
numbers 15:16 = 15:15
numbers 15:17-18 = 15:16-17

# numbers 27: the daughters' plea ends inside the Vulgate's 27:3
numbers 27:4 = 27:3
numbers 27:5-8 = 27:4-7

# 2 esdras (Nehemiah) 7: the Vulgate divides the Levites' list one verse later
2-esdras 7:44-47 = 7:45-48
2-esdras 7:48 = 7:48

# psalms 147: the Vulgate makes Psalm 147 of the Hebrew 147:12–20 ("Lauda,
# Jerusalem"); the Douay in this corpus numbers it 12–20, the Vulgate 1–9
psalms 147:12-20 = 147:1-9

# isaias 45: the Vulgate keeps the Hebrew division (24 "in Domino… justitiæ",
# 25 "in Domino justificabitur"); the Douay prints 26 verses, which misled the
# text check
isaias 45:24-25 = 45:24-25

# Where SIL's draft and TVTMS disagree, settled by reading the Latin:
# psalms 16 (Vulgate 15): the Clementine's 15:10 runs on into "notas mihi
# fecisti vias vitæ", the Hebrew 16:11
psalms 16:11 = 15:10
# psalms 116:10–19 is the Vulgate's Psalm 115 ("Credidi"), 1–10
psalms 116:10-19 = 115:1-10
# isaias 8:23 is the Vulgate's 9:1 ("Primo tempore alleviata est terra Zabulon")
isaias 8:23 = 9:1
# jonas 2: the Clementine's chapter 1 ends at 16, and 2:1 is the fish, as in the
# Hebrew (SIL sends 2:1 to 1:17)
jonas 2:1-11 = 2:1-11

# The 58 verses the text check left weak, each read beside the Douay: 52 are
# right (a boundary the Douay moves by a clause; the verse is kept where most of
# it stands). These six were not:
# exodus 38: the Vulgate tells the census before the silver — the half-shekel
# of the 603,550 numbered (Hebrew 26) is its 25, the hundred talents (25) its 26
exodus 38:25 = 38:26
exodus 38:26 = 38:25
# leviticus 15: the Vulgate makes a verse (20) of "every one that toucheth her",
# the end of the Hebrew 19, so her bed, its touching and her seat run one later
leviticus 15:20-22 = 15:21-23
# deuteronomy 6: "Take heed lest thou forget the Lord" is the Vulgate's 13; its
# 12 is the end of the Hebrew 11 ("and thou shalt have eaten and be full")
deuteronomy 6:12 = 6:13
# psalms 109 (Vulgate 108): "he loved cursing" and "he put on cursing like a
# garment" are both the Vulgate's 18; its 16–17 divide the Hebrew 16
psalms 109:17-18 = 108:18
# osee 6: the Vulgate's 6:1 opens with the end of the Hebrew 5:15, so "he will
# revive us after two days" and "let us know the Lord" are both its 6:3
osee 6:2-3 = 6:3
